</think>The astronaut’s experienced time is the proper time:

\[
\tau = \frac{d}{v\gamma}
\]

With \(v \approx c\), \(d \approx 160{,}000\) light-years, and

\[
\gamma = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-(0.99999987)^2}} \approx 1961
\]

\[
\tau \approx \frac{160{,}000}{1961} \approx 81.6 \text{ years}
\]

So the astronaut would be about \(22+81.6 \approx 103.6\) years old, still below the 150-year lifetime.

Answer: B
