This is likely from computer networking: throughput with window size. Sender transmits frames length 512 bytes = 4096 bits. Data rate 64 kb/s = 64000 bps? Note kb/s maybe kilobits/s. Transmission time Tf = 4096 / 64000 = 0.064 s = 64 ms. One-way propagation 270 ms. RTT = 540 ms. Ack negligible. For window size N, if N*Tf >= Tf + 2Tp? Actually sender can send N frames before waiting for ACK. Throughput = min(link rate, N*L / (Tf + 2Tp))? Let's derive.

Stop-and-wait N=1 throughput = L / (Tf + 2Tp) = 4096 bits / (0.064 + 0.54 s) = 4096 / 0.604 = 6781.5 bps = 6.78 kb/s. Choices include C first 6.8. Good.

For N=7: if window allows 7 frames per RTT, throughput = N*L / (Tf + 2Tp)? Wait denominator? For continuous sliding window, cycle to send N frames and receive ack for first? Let's derive.

Protocol: sender can transmit up to N unacknowledged frames. If N*Tf < RTT + Tf? Actually time to send N frames = N*Tf. Last frame arrives after Tf + Tp. ACK for first frame arrives after Tf + 2Tp from start of first frame. Sender can send next window after ACK for first. If N*Tf >= Tf + 2Tp, then ACK for first arrives before sender finishes sending N frames, so continuous, throughput = link rate. If N*Tf < Tf + 2Tp, sender stalls after N frames until ACK. Cycle time = Tf + 2Tp? Let's set start at t=0 sending frame 1. It takes Tf to transmit. ACK for frame 1 arrives at t=Tf+2Tp. During [0,N*Tf] send N frames. If N*Tf < Tf+2Tp, after N*Tf idle until t=Tf+2Tp, then can send frame N+1. Cycle for N frames? From start of frame 1 to start of frame N+1 = Tf+2Tp. Thus throughput = N*L / (Tf+2Tp). Yes.

For N=7: N*Tf = 7*64ms = 448ms. Tf+2Tp = 64+540=604ms. Throughput = 7*4096 / 0.604 = 28672 /0.604 = 47470 bps = 47.5 kb/s. Choice C second 47.5. For N=17: N*Tf = 1088ms >604, so link rate 64 kb/s. Choice C third 64. For N=117 also 64. So answer C.

