Atom 4: [C@@H]( ... ) - attached to atom 3, to a branch, and to the next atom [C@H]2 (atom 9). The branch is: [C@H]4[C@@H]3[C@@H]5C=C[C@H]4C5. Let's parse the branch.

Branch from atom 4: starts with [C@H]4 (atom 5). Atom 5 is attached to atom 4, to the next atom [C@@H]3 (atom 6), and has ring closure 4. Ring closure 4 will connect to a later atom (atom 8?).

Atom 6: [C@@H]3 - attached to atom 5, to the next atom [C@@H]5 (atom 7), and has ring closure 3. Ring closure 3 connects to atom 3. So atom 6 is bonded to atom 3.

Atom 7: [C@@H]5 - attached to atom 6, to the next atom C (atom 13), and has ring closure 5. Ring closure 5 will connect to a later atom (atom 15?).

Atom 13: C - attached to atom 7, double bonded to atom 14 (C). So atom 13 is double bonded to atom 14.

Atom 14: C - attached to atom 13, to the next atom [C@H]4 (atom 8). Atom 14 is double bonded to atom 13.

Atom 8: [C@H]4 - attached to atom 14, to the next atom C5 (atom 15), and has ring closure 4. Ring closure 4 connects to atom 5. So atom 8 is bonded to atom 5.

Atom 15: C5 - attached to atom 8, and has ring closure 5. Ring closure 5 connects to atom 7. So atom 15 is bonded to atom 7. Atom 15 is a CH2? It's written as C5, so it's a carbon with two hydrogens? Actually in SMILES, C5 means an aliphatic carbon with ring closure 5. It is attached to atom 8 and atom 7. So it's a CH2 bridge.

